      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Blanch the bones: put the beef bones in a stockpot and cover with cold water. Bring to a boil, cook for 5 minutes, then drain. Rinse the bones and the pot well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Char the aromatics: hold the onion halves and ginger directly over a gas flame with tongs, or place them under a hot grill, until blackened in spots . about 3 to 4 minutes. This is the charring step."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Build the broth: return the blanched bones to the stockpot with 3 litres of cold water. Add the charred onion, ginger, star anise, cinnamon stick, cloves, and cardamom. Bring to a boil, then reduce to a gentle simmer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the brisket to the pot after 30 minutes. Continue simmering for a further 1 hour 30 minutes, or until the brisket is tender. Skim any foam that rises to the surface."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the brisket and set aside to cool slightly, then slice thinly. Strain the broth through a fine sieve and discard the solids. Return the broth to the pot."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season the broth with fish sauce, sugar, and salt to taste. Keep at a rolling simmer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook the noodles according to the packet instructions, then divide between 4 deep bowls. Top with sliced brisket and raw sirloin."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Ladle the boiling broth over the bowls . the heat will cook the raw sirloin. Serve immediately with bean sprouts, spring onion, coriander, Thai basil, lime wedges, and chilli on the side."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Broth is cloudy",
              "cause": "The bones were not blanched, or the broth was boiled hard rather than simmered.",
              "fix": "Always blanch and rinse the bones first. Keep the heat low . the surface should just tremble, not bubble."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Broth tastes flat",
              "cause": "Not enough fish sauce or the spices were old.",
              "fix": "Add fish sauce a tablespoon at a time. Toast the whole spices in a dry pan for 1 minute before adding them to the pot."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Raw sirloin is not cooked through",
              "cause": "The broth was not hot enough when ladled over.",
              "fix": "Make sure the broth is at a full simmer . not just warm . when you ladle it. Use a very thin slice of beef."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pho bo is a northern Vietnamese dish that most likely started in Nam Dinh province in the early 1900s. It became the signature dish of Hanoi. After Vietnam was divided in 1954, many northerners brought the dish south to Saigon. The southern version became sweeter and came with more garnishes on the side. Today "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "pho"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "pho "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "is eaten for breakfast and dinner across Vietnam. It is also found wherever Vietnamese communities have settled around the world."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
